.SH "NAME"
nvme-keys-gen-tls \- Generate a NVMe TLS PSK
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fInvme\fR [<global\-options>] \fIkeys gen\-tls\fR [\-\-keyring=<name> | \-k <name>]
                        [\-\-keytype=<type> | \-t <type>]
                        [\-\-hostnqn=<nqn> | \-n <nqn>]
                        [\-\-subsysnqn=<nqn> | \-c <nqn>]
                        [\-\-hmac=<hmac\-id> | \-m <hmac\-id>]
                        [\-\-identity=<id\-vers> | \-I <id\-vers>]
                        [\-\-secret=<secret> | \-s <secret>]
                        [\-\-insert | \-i]
                        [\-\-compat | \-C]
                        [\-\-keyfile=<keyfile> | \-f <keyfile>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Generate a base64\-encoded NVMe TLS pre\-shared key (PSK)\&. The resulting key is either printed in the PSK interchange format \fINVMeTLSkey\-1:01:<base64 encoded data>:\fR or inserted as a \fIretained\fR key into the specified keyring if the \fI\-\-insert\fR option is given\&. When the PSK should be inserted into the keyring a \fIretained\fR key is derived from the secret key material using the HKDF\-Expand\-Label algorithm from RFC 8446\&. The resulting \fIretained\fR key is stored with the identity \fINVMe0R0<hmac> <host NQN> <subsystem NQN>\fR (for identity version \fI0\fR) or \fINVMe1R0<hmac> <host NQN> <subsystem NQN> <PSK hash>\fR (for identity version \fI1\fR) in the keyring\&. The \fIretained\fR key is derived from the secret key material, the specified subsystem NQN, and the host NQN\&. Once the \fIretained\fR key is stored in the keyring the original secret key material cannot be retrieved\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\-k <name>, \-\-keyring=<name>
.RS 4
Name of the keyring into which the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key should be stored\&. Default is
\fI\&.nvme\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-t <type>, \-\-keytype=<type>
.RS 4
Type of the key for resulting TLS key\&. Default is
\fIpsk\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\-n <nqn>, \-\-hostnqn=<nqn>
.RS 4
Host NVMe Qualified Name (NQN) to be used to derive the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key
.RE
.PP
\-c <nqn>, \-\-subsysnqn=<nqn>
.RS 4
Subsystem NVMe Qualified Name (NQN) to be used to derive the
\fIretained\fR
TLS key
.RE
.PP
\-m <hmac\-id>, \-\-hmac=<hmac\-id>
.RS 4
Select a HMAC algorithm to use\&. Possible values are: 1 \- SHA\-256 (default) 2 \- SHA\-384
.RE
.PP
\-I <vers>, \-\-identity=<id\-vers>
.RS 4
Select the TLS identity to use\&. Possible values are: 0 \- Original NVMe TLS 1\&.0c identity 1 \- NVMe TLS 2\&.0 (TP8018) identity
.RE
.PP
\-s <secret>, \-\-secret=<secret>
.RS 4
Secret value (in hexadecimal) to be used for the key\&. If none are provided a random value is used\&.
.RE
.PP
\-i, \-\-insert
.RS 4
Insert the resulting TLS key into the keyring without printing out the key in PSK interchange format\&.
.RE
.PP
\-C, \-\-compat
.RS 4
Use the original non\-RFC 8446 compliant algorithm when deriving TLS keys for compatibility with older implementations\&.
.RE
.PP
\-f <keyfile>, \-\-keyfile=<keyfile>
.RS 4
Append the resulting TLS key to keyfile\&. This command line option depends on \-\-insert\&.
.RE
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.sp
The following options are defined at the top\-level nvme command and are available to this subcommand:
.PP
\-\-dry\-run
.RS 4
Print the command that would be executed, but do not actually execute it\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-no\-ioctl\-probing
.RS 4
Disable probing for 64\-bit IOCTL support\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-no\-retries
.RS 4
Disable retry logic on transient errors\&.
.RE
.PP
\-o <fmt>, \-\-output\-format=<fmt>
.RS 4
Set the reporting format to
\fInormal\fR,
\fItabular, \*(Aqjson\fR, or
\fIbinary\fR\&. Only one output format may be used at a time\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-output\-format\-version=<version>
.RS 4
Select the output format version\&. Version
\fI1\fR
uses the original field naming, while version
\fI2\fR
(default) provides more consistent and script\-friendly field names\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-timeout=<ms>
.RS 4
Set the timeout for the command in milliseconds\&.
.RE
.PP
\-v, \-\-verbose
.RS 4
Increase the level of detail in the output\&. May be specified multiple times to further increase verbosity\&.
.RE
.sp
These options can also be set as machine\-wide defaults in nvme\-cli\&.conf(5)\&. A command\-line flag always overrides the file\&.
